{
  "answer": "For an HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect on the same hostname, Requests keeps the Authorization header only when the original URL uses port 80 (or no explicit port) and the redirected URL uses port 443 (or no explicit port). In that case, should_strip_auth() returns false. For example, http://example.com -> https://example.com preserves Authorization. If either side uses a non-standard port, the special case does not apply; because the scheme changed, the final logic returns true and strips the header. Redirects to a different hostname always strip it. HTTPAdapter.send() does not make this decision; redirect handling in SessionRedirectMixin calls should_strip_auth() before sending the next request. HTTPDigestAuth.handle_401() is separate and handles Digest authentication challenges, not this redirect policy.",
  "file": "sessions.py (SessionRedirectMixin.should_strip_auth), lines 127-157; adapters.py (HTTPAdapter.send), lines 613-719; auth.py (HTTPDigestAuth.handle_401), lines 241-283",
  "line": "The HTTP-to-HTTPS exception is implemented at sessions.py lines 133-143. The fallback scheme/port comparison is at lines 145-157."
}

{
  "answer": "Requests reads the Location header, decodes it, and resolves it against the URL of the response being redirected. A scheme-relative Location such as //example.com/path inherits the original response's scheme. For other relative locations, Requests percent-encodes the URI and uses urljoin(resp.url, requote_uri(url)). Thus, /next resolves from the origin root, next resolves relative to the current response path, and ../next resolves against the current directory. If the Location has no fragment, the previous URL fragment is preserved; an explicitly supplied fragment replaces it. The resulting URL is assigned to the copied PreparedRequest, after which Requests rebuilds method, headers, cookies, authentication, proxies, and possibly the request body before sending the redirect.",
  "file": "requests/sessions.py",
  "line": "198-219 (relative-URL processing); 107-124 (reading and decoding Location)"
}

{
  "answer": "Requests treats HTTP header names case-insensitively. `Content-Type`, `content-type`, and other casing variants refer to the same header because request headers use a `CaseInsensitiveDict`. Therefore, `headers.pop(\"Content-Type\", None)` also removes a header originally supplied as `content-type`. The value is associated with one normalized case-insensitive key, while the most recently assigned spelling may be preserved for output.",
  "file": "requests/sessions.py",
  "line": "229-231"
}

{
  "answer": "Requests converts the URL to a string, strips leading whitespace, and parses it into its components. It validates the scheme and host, IDNA-encodes non-ASCII hostnames, ensures a path exists, and encodes the supplied `params` using `_encode_params`. If the original URL already has a query string, the encoded parameters are appended with `&`; otherwise they become the query string. Finally, it reconstructs and requotes the URL, storing the result in `self.url`. The request is then prepared and sent using this final URL.",
