{
  "answer": "When you pass a Python object using the `json=` parameter, Requests serializes it with `json.dumps()` (via `complexjson.dumps`), encodes the resulting JSON text as UTF-8 bytes, and sets `Content-Type: application/json` unless you provide that header yourself. For example, `requests.post(url, json={\"a\": 1})` sends the body `{\"a\": 1}`. The `_encode_files` method is only used for multipart requests involving `files=`. If you pass `data=` instead, Requests does not JSON-serialize the value automatically; a string or bytes value is sent as provided.",
  "file": "requests/models.py",
  "line": "PreparedRequest.prepare_body, approximately lines 500–518"
}

